# Always Build + Relaunch After Code Changes

When you modify any Swift file in this project, you MUST do the full build → kill → launch cycle before handing the turn back to the user. A successful build alone is not enough: any already-running `Dogbody.app` instance keeps running the *old* binary, so the user won't see your fix until you relaunch.

## The full cycle

Run from the repo root, in this order:

### 1) Build to a fixed path we own

```bash
xcodebuild -project Dogbody.xcodeproj -scheme Dogbody -configuration Debug \
  -derivedDataPath .build/derived \
  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY="-" CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO build \
  2>&1 | rg "BUILD|error:|warning:" | rg -v "xcodebuild: WARNING"
```

The grep filter keeps output short: only `BUILD SUCCEEDED` / `BUILD FAILED`, errors, and real warnings (excluding xcodebuild's own destination chatter). Do NOT use Xcode's `~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData/...` path — Xcode will clobber it on its own builds.

If `project.yml` was edited, regenerate first: `xcodegen generate`.

### 2) Kill any running instance (Xcode-launched or otherwise)

```bash
pkill -9 -f "Dogbody.app/Contents/MacOS/Dogbody" 2>/dev/null; sleep 1
```

There may be a Xcode-launched binary at `~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData/Dogbody-<hash>/Build/Products/Debug/Dogbody.app/...`. Kill it too — `pkill` matches all of them.

### 3) Launch the freshly built version

```bash
open .build/derived/Build/Products/Debug/Dogbody.app
sleep 1
pgrep -lf "Dogbody.app/Contents/MacOS"
```

The `pgrep` line confirms the new process is running and shows the path — verify it points to `.build/derived/...`, not Xcode's DerivedData.

## Rules

- Run the full cycle (build → kill → launch) at the end of every code change, before responding.
- If the build fails, fix it before reporting back. Don't hand a broken build to the user.
- If the build only emits real warnings (not the `xcodebuild: WARNING: Using the first of multiple matching destinations` line), surface them in your reply.
- Don't run `./scripts/build.sh` — it has a `set -u` + empty-array bug; call `xcodebuild` directly.
- A common failure mode: build succeeds, but the user is still looking at an old running instance. ALWAYS kill + relaunch, even if you "just did it last turn."
